   "ds": "",
   "x": 60,
   "y": 60,
   "text": "Example: Ethernet switch – three PCs on one 8-port switch (SW1), 192.168.1.0 / 24",
   "size": 18,
   "bold": 1,
   "it": 0,
   "al": "left",
   "rot": 0
  },
  {
   "id": 202,
   "k": "text",
   "pg": 1,
   "all": 0,
   "col": "",
   "wd": 2,
   "ds": "",
   "x": 60,
   "y": 78,
   "text": "Press Run: PC1 and PC3 send pings. The first exchange is an ARP broadcast (orange) that the switch floods to every port; then the frames (blue = data, green = reply) go only to the learned port. Select SW1 to see its MAC table.",
   "size": 12,
   "bold": 0,
   "it": 0,
   "al": "left",
   "rot": 0
  },
  {
   "id": 203,
   "k": "text",
   "pg": 1,
   "all": 0,
   "col": "",
   "wd": 2,
   "ds": "",
   "x": 60,
   "y": 96,
   "text": "Each device: LAN → RJ45 PORT … CABLE → cable → CABLE … PORT → switch port. Ports used: 1 (PC1), 4 (PC3), 7 (PC2). Straight-through cables, both plugs T568B.",
   "size": 12,
   "bold": 0,
   "it": 0,
   "al": "left",
   "rot": 0
  },
  {
   "id": 204,
   "k": "text",
   "pg": 1,
   "all": 0,
   "col": "",
   "wd": 2,
   "ds": "",
   "x": 60,
   "y": 114,
   "text": "Break it: give PC3 the address 192.168.2.30 (other subnet – needs a router); give PC2 the same IP as PC1 (conflict); add a cable between two free switch ports (ring = broadcast storm); make CAB3 120 m; set Number of ports to 5 and use port 7.",
   "size": 12,
   "bold": 0,
   "it": 0,
   "al": "left",
   "rot": 0
  }
 ],
